# Line endings.
#
# The repository stores LF. Without this file, editing on Windows rewrites files
# to CRLF and git reports every line of every file as changed: `git diff --check`
# produced 16,978 whitespace errors and `git diff --stat` claimed roughly 78 files
# and 10,000 changed lines when the real change was 41 files. That makes review
# impossible and hides genuine changes inside the noise.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect text files and normalise them to LF on the way into
# the index; `eol=lf` checks them back out as LF so the divergence cannot reappear.
# Adding this file alone dropped the phantom diff to 120 lines without rewriting
# anything in the working tree.
#
# One-time cleanup, best run immediately after committing unrelated work so the
# two do not mix:
#
#   git add --renormalize .
#   git commit -m "Normalise line endings to LF"
#
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly binary. `text=auto` detects these correctly in practice, but naming
# them means a corrupt font or image can never be attributed to line-ending
# translation.
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
*.ttf    binary
*.otf    binary
*.eot    binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.webp   binary
*.avif   binary
*.ico    binary
*.pdf    binary

# SVG is text and benefits from real diffs.
*.svg    text eol=lf

# Generated. Marking these keeps merge conflicts in them from being hand-edited.
package-lock.json  -diff linguist-generated
*.tsbuildinfo      -diff linguist-generated
